call_details.response:
The current position is in the opening phase, with no immediate wins available for either player (verified by exhaustive checks of all seven possible drops). Player o already has two pieces aligned on the diagonal occupying (5,2) and (4,3). The unique extension of this pair that produces three-in-a-row is the square (3,4). Occupying that square simultaneously:

- converts the diagonal into a sleeping three (the fourth cell (2,5) is not yet playable);
- claims nine still-open 4-in-row groups, two of which already contain a second o;
- prevents x from using the same square to create an immediate vertical threat of three (which would have forced an answer on the subsequent move) and from blocking o’s only advanced line.

The rival central square (3,3) offers a slightly higher raw count of open lines but yields only a single two-in-a-row and leaves the vital diagonal unprotected. Consequently x would answer on (3,4), killing o’s best asset while seizing the initiative. All other columns produce at most isolated pairs and cede the same key square. Column 4 is therefore the unique move that both develops o’s strongest formation and denies x a comparable formation.

Final Answer: 4

